### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer wants your incident-response discipline under pressure. They care about the method, not just the fix. A calm, step-by-step account with evidence and prevention signals a senior engineer. A panicked narrative with no prevention signals a junior one.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class IncidentDiscipline {
    enum Level { SENIOR, JUNIOR }

    public static Level assess(boolean stabilizedFirst, boolean usedEvidence, boolean addedPrevention) {
        return (stabilizedFirst && usedEvidence && addedPrevention) ? Level.SENIOR : Level.JUNIOR;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(assess(true, true, true));   // SENIOR
        System.out.println(assess(false, false, false)); // JUNIOR
    }
}
```

### How do you detect and triage?

Detection comes from an alert, a customer report, or a metric anomaly. Triage quantifies impact: "every transfer was failing, affecting all customers at National Bank of India."

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class IncidentDetection {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(IncidentDetection.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double errorRate = 95.0;
        int affectedCustomers = 50000;
        log.severe(() -> "ALERT: Transfer error rate=" + errorRate + "% affecting " + affectedCustomers + " customers");
        log.info("Triage: SEV1 - all transfers blocked");
    }
}
```

### How do you stabilize before investigating?

Stabilization is a safe, reversible action that reduces harm. A feature flag or circuit breaker lets you cut off a failing path fast, even before you know the root cause.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class Stabilization {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(Stabilization.class.getName());
    private static boolean newTransferPathEnabled = true;

    public static void stabilize() {
        newTransferPathEnabled = false; // flip feature flag
        log.info("Stabilized: disabled new transfer path, using legacy fallback");
        log.info("Customers can transfer again while we investigate");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        stabilize();
    }
}
```

```text
Stabilized: disabled new transfer path, using legacy fallback
Customers can transfer again while we investigate
```

### How do you investigate with evidence?

Use logs, metrics, traces, and thread dumps to narrow from symptom to cause. Rising latency plus growing database connections points to connection-pool exhaustion. A thread dump with many threads BLOCKED on the same lock points to a deadlock.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class EvidenceInvestigation {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(EvidenceInvestigation.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulated investigation
        log.info("Metrics: DB connection pool = 50/50 (exhausted)");
        log.info("Logs: retry count per request = unbounded");
        log.info("Diagnosis: retry storm exhausted connection pool");
        log.info("Root cause: no retry budget; transient DB slowdown triggered unbounded retries");
    }
}
```

### How do you fix and verify?

The fix targets the root cause. Verification uses the same metrics you used to diagnose. Both must happen before you declare the incident resolved.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class FixAndVerify {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(FixAndVerify.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Fix: capped retries at 3 with exponential backoff");
        log.info("Fix: drained and recovered connection pool");
        log.info("Verify: pool usage back to 12/50 (healthy)");
        log.info("Verify: transfer success rate back to 99.8%");
    }
}
```

### How do you communicate throughout?

Send clear status updates every few minutes so stakeholders can make business decisions. This is as important as the technical fix.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class IncidentComms {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(IncidentComms.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("[12:01] Incident detected: transfers failing at 95% error rate");
        log.info("[12:03] Stabilized: legacy fallback active, transfers resuming");
        log.info("[12:15] Root cause identified: retry storm on DB pool");
        log.info("[12:25] Fix deployed: retries capped, pool recovering");
        log.info("[12:30] Resolved: error rate back to normal");
    }
}
```

### How do you add prevention?

A blameless postmortem leads to concrete preventive actions: a new alert, a test, a config limit. "This class of problem can never again build up silently."

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class Prevention {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(Prevention.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Prevention 1: Alert on pool usage > 80%");
        log.info("Prevention 2: Retry budget monitor with circuit breaker");
        log.info("Prevention 3: Load test covering retry storms in CI");
        log.info("Class of problem: can no longer build up silently");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer emphasize about the postmortem?

Focus on the system and the process, not on who made a mistake. A blameless postmortem improves the system; a blame-heavy one only makes people hide mistakes.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class BlamelessPostmortem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Blameless: 'The system allowed unbounded retries' -> fix the system");
        System.out.println("Blame: 'Developer X forgot to add a limit' -> people hide mistakes");
        System.out.println("Always focus on systemic fixes, not individual blame.");
    }
}
```
